    "text": "Standard bidirectional video diffusion models' performance degrades as the causal chain length of multi-ball hard-sphere dynamics increases, even with more denoising steps.",
    "topic": "Seriality Gap in Video Diffusion Models"
  },
  {
    "text": "When ball-ball interactions are absent in a length-matched single-ball control, the performance degradation observed in multi-ball experiments largely disappears.",
    "topic": "Seriality Gap in Video Diffusion Models"
  },
  {
    "text": "The degradation in video diffusion model performance with longer causal chains is primarily due to dependent-event structure rather than video length itself.",
    "topic": "Seriality Gap in Video Diffusion Models"
  },
  {
    "text": "For deterministic video prediction, additional denoising steps in video diffusion models do not contribute to serial computation beyond the backbone network, indicating a structural limitation for serial reasoning tasks.",
    "topic": "Memory in Video Diffusion Models"
  },
  {
    "text": "Recent video reconstruction from sparse event streams often suffers from blurred textures and poor long-term stability, while few-step autoregressive video generation accumulates errors and weakens motion dynamics over extended sequences.",
    "topic": "Memory in Video Diffusion Models"
  }
]
